> from the point of view of an "unspoiled" player:
> 
> Arrive at Oracle, pay, Oracle says you can tell what rings are what by
> dumping down a drain. GREAT!  So just how do you tell?!?!?  Some are
> obvious, but, since you DON'T EVEN KNOW what rings are available, how
> are you to tell what a ring is by the effect?
> 
> There are similar problems with armor, etc.
> 
Come on, I started playing HACK about 6 years ago (ST HACK 1.03), then
I played 1.06(?), NH 2.2, 2.3, some 'hacked up' variants of 2.x, then
3.0, THEN I discovered r.g.h and got spoilers. I never managed to finish
the game before that point, but I managed to get quite deep and I've seen
the amulet (don't remember in which version any more). And I managed to
discover quite a number of things on my own (including at least some of
ring-sink messages meaning). Sure, after I read the spoilers I found that
I didn't know about 75% of the game features - but that was part of the
fun, knowing that there are things in the game I still have to discover.
And I found at that time the game pretty well balanced on all levels -
i.e. going up in levels didn't decrease my chance of dying much... In 
fact I'm still wondering how did the authors manage to produce such well
balanced (through difficult) game KNOWING all the tricks...

If 3.1 forces you to use spoilers, that is because either
- dev team was under too much pressure from spoiled players and made
  it too tough, or
- you should not be playing nethack, as you are not in for this kind
  of games.
I'm now also a spoiled player, so I can't in the moment tell which one 
of the above is true ;-)

Btw: when 3.1 came out I vowed not to read any spoiler messages on r.g.h
until my first ascension. It didn't work, as some messages didn't have
spoiler warnings on them, and sometimes even message titles were spoilers
("How to find the vibrating square?"). If ever there will be Nethack 3.2
(or 4.0), I promise that the announcement will be the last thing I read
on r.g.h until ascension ;-)

> I guess my point is that the game is complicated enough that it needs
> SIZABLE documentation.  Since this documentation does not exsist, the spoiler
> files are the only things left.
> 
Yes, it needs a kind of 'background' documentation, describing the world
you live in in general, and the guidebook (you read it, didn't you?) is
a good background (one version even mentioned Elbereth...). Maybe it
could be expanded a bit (more info on character classes, stats and their
effects, some weapon table instedad of AD&D reference - this all should
be a 'common  knowledge' in a nethack world) - but not much more than is
already there.

> I think that one of the BIG unbalances in the game is wishes.  Wishes for
> artifacts should always be granted in the form of a suitably strong monster
> wielding or carring it.  Ask for Dragonbane, get a Black Dragon, etc.  This
> would slow down the acquisition of artifacts to a more reasonable pace.
> Something also MUST be done about Yendorian Express--I've gone up about
> 7 levels next to alters with it + create monster wand, and aquired about
> 5 artifact weapons doing it (still waiting for vorpy).  By the time I hit
> the quest, I should be around 20th--I don't anticipate much trouble.
> 
For unspoiled players wishes are not such an unbalance. I, for one,
never discovered that I could wish for 'blessed' objects or multiple
scrolls... And how long do you thing wold it take you to find all the
benefits of the Yendorian Express card without reading that artifact
list here? In fact how long would it take you to discover that there
EXISTS a yendorian expess card in the game? Tourists are quite difficult
to play. BUT - I agree, whishing for quest artifacts should be forbidden
(Wizard wishing for Orb of Fate? no, nothing fitting the descripion
exists in the game, it's a wizard game after all...)

I have a good challenge for you - finish the game without using wishes
at all if it is too unbalancing for you. In 3.0 I once tried for fun (and
succeeded) to finish the game without polypiling, ring of conflict and
robbing shops, because I thought these were unbalancing the game too much...
Of course I was already badly spoiled at that point ;-)

